WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:30.000
This is 99% Invisible. I'm Roman Mars. In August 2024, James Daniels was suiting up for an NFL preseason game. Daniels was an offensive lineman for the Pittsburgh Steelers, and he was signed to a lucrative three-year contract to play with the team. But that day, nobody was talking about Daniels as a player. The news media was focused on Daniels because of what he was wearing. That's producer Chris Rube.
这里是《99%隐形》。我是罗曼·马尔斯。2024年8月，詹姆斯·丹尼尔斯正在为一场NFL季前赛做准备。丹尼尔斯是匹兹堡钢人队的一名进攻锋线球员，他刚与球队签下了一份丰厚的三年代言合同。但那天，没人谈论丹尼尔斯作为球员的表现。新闻媒体关注丹尼尔斯是因为他戴的东西。那是制片人克里斯·鲁贝。

2
00:00:30.000 --> 00:01:03.000
Daniels was believed to be the first NFL player, to sport a new kind of helmet during a game. An awkward piece of equipment called a guardian cap. A guardian cap is basically an extra layer of padding that goes around the outside of a traditional football helmet. So you've got your helmet and then you've got your guardian cap. And then you've got another layer that you pull down over the guardian cap that then represents the logos of the team, the Chargers, lightning bolts or the Green Bay G or whatever it may be. Noah Cohen is a professor of American Culture Studies at Washington University in St. Louis.
据信，丹尼尔斯是第一位在比赛中佩戴一种新型头盔的NFL球员。这是一种笨拙的装备，叫做“守护者帽”（Guardian cap）。守护者帽本质上是在传统橄榄球头盔外层增加的一层衬垫。所以你先戴上头盔，然后再戴上守护者帽。接着，你还要在守护者帽外面套上一层外罩，上面印有球队的标志，比如闪电队的闪电标志、绿湾包装工队的G字母，或者其他任何标志。诺亚·科恩是圣路易斯华盛顿大学的美国文化研究教授。

3
00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:38.000
and he's the author of an upcoming book about football helmets. He says the Guardian cap looks markedly different from what football fans are used to. It sort of looks like if you had soundproof padding in the form of a cap and stretch that over your helmet. It's a series of lumps made of foam within a sort of fabric covering that's then clipped to the outside of the helmet. The Guardian cap is meant to absorb, the impact of headshots and to protect players' brains from concussions.
他是一本即将出版的关于橄榄球头盔书籍的作者。他说，守护者帽看起来与橄榄球迷们习惯看到的样子截然不同。它看起来就像是你把隔音衬垫做成帽子的形状，然后套在头盔上。它是一系列由泡沫制成的肿块，包裹在某种织物覆盖层内，然后扣在头盔外面。守护者帽旨在吸收头部撞击的冲击力，保护球员的大脑免受脑震荡。

4
00:01:38.000 --> 00:02:12.000
Some people hailed the caps as a big step for player safety. Today, most NFL players are required to wear them during practices, and some players even wear them during NFL games. But since their introduction, players wearing the Guardian caps have received a ton of mockery. The caps make players' helmets look bulbous and disproportionate to the rest of their body. They make professional athletes look like human bobbleheads. Your head is huge. You look like the Great Gazoo. It's very noticeable.
有些人称赞这种帽子是球员安全方面的一大进步。如今，大多数NFL球员在训练时被要求佩戴它们，有些球员甚至在NFL比赛中也佩戴。但自推出以来，佩戴守护者帽的球员遭到了大量的嘲笑。这种帽子让球员的头盔看起来臃肿且与身体其他部分不成比例。它们让职业运动员看起来像真人版的摇头娃娃。你的头太大了。你看起来像《杰森一家》里的“伟大的加祖”。这非常显眼。

5
00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:41.000
It's not just the fans who take issue with Guardian caps. Many players complain about sacrificing swagger for safety. Philadelphia Eagles star Jason Kelsey actually made fun of the whole idea of a Guardian cap during a practice by covering his head in bubble wrap. Other players have said they have no plans. to wear them during games. Like former Miami Dolphins quarterback Tua Tungo Viloa, who suffered multiple concussions, here he is in a press conference in 2024.
不仅仅是球迷对守护者帽有意见。许多球员抱怨为了安全而牺牲了风度。费城老鹰队的明星杰森·凯尔西在一次训练中，甚至用气泡膜包裹住头部，以此来嘲笑守护者帽的整个概念。其他球员也表示，他们没有计划在比赛中佩戴它们。比如前迈阿密海豚队四分卫图阿·塔戈瓦伊洛阿，他曾多次遭受脑震荡，这是他在2024年新闻发布会上的发言。

6
00:02:41.000 --> 00:03:03.000
Will you wear a guardian cap? Nope. Why? Personal choice. Most players will agree that, yes, it is a good thing to develop new football helmets that make the game safer, but they aren't embracing the guardian cap. In fact, pretty much every evolution of the football helmet has received a mixed response from players and fans.
你会戴守护者帽吗？不会。为什么？个人选择。大多数球员都会同意，开发能让比赛更安全的新型橄榄球头盔确实是件好事，但他们并不接受守护者帽。事实上，橄榄球头盔的每一次演变几乎都受到了球员和球迷的褒贬不一的评价。

7
00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:28.000
Football helmets have been a flashpoint between folks who want to make football safer and those who want to keep the game rough and tumble. It's been at the center of moral panics and court cases. And today, it's part of a bigger discussion about whether or not it's responsible or even ethical to keep playing America's most popular sport. It may be hard to believe,
橄榄球头盔一直是那些希望让橄榄球更安全的人与那些希望保持比赛粗犷激烈风格的人之间的冲突焦点。它一直处于道德恐慌和法律诉讼的中心。而今天，它已成为关于继续进行美国最受欢迎的运动是否负责任，甚至是否合乎道德的更大讨论的一部分。这可能很难相信，

8
00:03:28.000 --> 00:04:07.000
but there was a time before football was America's game. Way back in the 18th, In the 1970s, football was mostly a distraction for rowdy college students. The very first sort of intercollegiate, organized match between Rutgers and Princeton, I believe, is more like a soccer match than what we understand today as American football. Ivy League college students started mixing soccer and rugby to make up a new kind of American super sport. Originally, American football had a lot of, you know, using your feet to kick the ball forward. But that changed after a fateful game between Harvard University
但在橄榄球成为“美国运动”之前，曾有过一段时期。早在18世纪70年代，橄榄球主要只是吵闹的大学生的消遣。据我所知，罗格斯大学和普林斯顿大学之间举行的第一场校际组织比赛，与其说是我们今天所理解的美国橄榄球，不如说更像是一场足球比赛。常春藤盟校的大学生开始将足球和橄榄球混合起来，创造出一种新的美国超级运动。最初，美国橄榄球有很多用脚向前踢球的动作。但在哈佛大学与

9
00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:38.000
and some good old Canadians from McGill University in Montreal. Like most new sports, there was a certain fluidity to the rules in style of play. Harvard was used to a version of football with a lot of kicking, while McGill was still playing a version of rugby, where the ball was passed around by hand. So the Canadians saw what Harvard was doing and said, hey, why are you kicking that ball, you absolute losers? And McGill says you shouldn't play that kicking game.
蒙特利尔麦吉尔大学的一些加拿大老友之间进行了一场决定性的比赛后，情况发生了变化。像大多数新运动一样，规则和比赛风格有一定的流动性。哈佛大学习惯于一种有很多踢球动作的橄榄球版本，而麦吉尔大学当时还在玩一种橄榄球版本，球是用手传球的。所以加拿大人看到哈佛的做法后说，嘿，你们这些彻头彻尾的失败者，为什么要踢那个球？麦吉尔大学说你们不应该玩那种踢球的游戏。

10
00:04:38.000 --> 00:05:00.000
What you want to play is this rugby game where you carry the ball. It's more masculine. It's better. That was the first sort of sliding doors moment where we get to American football. Even after football largely got rid of kicking, it was still a very different game from what we're familiar with today. There was no forward passing. Players would huck the ball backwards like they do in rugby.
你们想玩的是这种用手持球的橄榄球游戏。它更阳刚。它更好。那是我们通往美国橄榄球之路的第一个“滑动门”时刻。即使在橄榄球很大程度上摆脱了踢球动作之后，它仍然与我们今天熟悉的比赛非常不同。当时没有向前传球。球员们会像在橄榄球比赛中那样把球向后扔。

11
00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:38.000
But the biggest difference might be how the game. the players looked. In fact, early football stars didn't look like athletes at all. Yeah, I mean, they're literally wearing sweaters and what they would have called knickers, you know, heavy cloth pants and high socks, some kind of early cleated shoe, probably. But they weren't wearing pads. These sweaters and knickers didn't offer a ton of physical protection, which was a problem, because even in the early days, football was quite violent. Right from the start, football players were tackling each other, hard.
但最大的区别可能在于比赛和球员的样子。事实上，早期的橄榄球明星看起来一点也不像运动员。是的，我的意思是，他们穿着毛衣和他们所谓的灯笼裤，你知道，厚布裤子和高筒袜，可能还有某种早期的钉鞋。但他们没有穿护具。这些毛衣和灯笼裤并没有提供太多的物理保护，这成了一个问题，因为即使在早期，橄榄球也相当暴力。从一开始，橄榄球运动员就在猛烈地互相冲撞。

12
00:05:38.000 --> 00:06:02.000
Players might break a rib and then they might stuff something in their sweater, or if they broke their nose. I mean, I've even seen pictures of almost like a spoon attached to a headband to sort of brace a nose. So at first, there really wasn't anything. People kept hitting their heads. I mean, people died. Despite the violence, or perhaps because of the violence, football became a national obsession in the late 19th century.
球员可能会断一根肋骨，然后他们可能会在毛衣里塞点东西，或者如果他们鼻子断了。我的意思是，我甚至见过照片，几乎像是一个勺子连接在头带上，用来支撑鼻子。所以起初，真的什么都没有。人们不断地撞击头部。我的意思是，有人死了。尽管有暴力，或者也许正是因为暴力，橄榄球在19世纪末成为了全国性的痴迷。

13
00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:51.000
But this rise to prominence also set off a national debate about whether it was responsible to let smart young men play such a barbaric game. There were frequent calls to stop playing football, even from people like Charles Elliott, the president of Harvard University, wanted his university to stop playing football at around the time of the turn of the century. But this sport was already so popular that his wanting this couldn't affect that change, even at his own university. This problem hit a crisis level in 1905. That year, 19 football players died at the high school and college levels. These were all young, healthy men. There were calls to ban the sport, which got so loud, President Roosevelt had to intervene. Teddy Roosevelt loved football.
但这种声望的上升也引发了一场全国性的辩论，讨论让聪明的年轻人玩这种野蛮的游戏是否负责任。经常有人呼吁停止橄榄球运动，甚至像哈佛大学校长查尔斯·艾略特这样的人，在世纪之交左右也希望他的大学停止橄榄球比赛。但这项运动已经太受欢迎了，以至于他的意愿无法改变这一点，即使在他自己的大学里也是如此。这个问题在1905年达到了危机水平。那一年，有19名高中和大学水平的橄榄球运动员死亡。他们都是年轻、健康的男性。要求禁止这项运动的呼声非常高，以至于罗斯福总统不得不进行干预。泰迪·罗斯福热爱橄榄球。

14
00:06:51.000 --> 00:07:28.000
In one letter to his son, Roosevelt encouraged Ted Jr. to play the game despite the risks, saying, the chance of your damaging yourself in body, is outweighed by the possibility of bitterness of spirit if you don't play. But President Roosevelt also knew the violence couldn't keep going at this level. He felt a lot of pressure to save the game from itself. In 1905, Teddy Roosevelt convened an emergency meeting at the White House, and there, a group of coaches and officials devised a plan to reform football. They created the first national body to govern university athletics,
在写给儿子的一封信中，罗斯福鼓励小泰德尽管有风险也要参加比赛，他说，如果你不参加比赛，精神上的痛苦远大于你身体受损的可能性。但罗斯福总统也知道暴力不能再维持在这个水平了。他感到拯救这项运动免于自我毁灭的巨大压力。1905年，泰迪·罗斯福在白宫召集了一次紧急会议，在那里，一群教练和官员制定了一项改革橄榄球的计划。他们创建了第一个管理大学体育的全国性机构，

15
00:07:28.000 --> 00:07:57.000
a body that would become the NCAA. They introduced a whole raft of rule changes, including the forward pass as a way to cut down on tackling and avoid violent pile-ups. And slowly, safety equipment became a standard part of football. Players started to wear shoulder pads and face guards, and some intrepid person came up with the first football helmet, though it's not actually clear who that was.
一个后来成为NCAA的机构。他们引入了一整套规则变更，包括向前传球，作为减少冲撞和避免暴力堆叠的一种方式。慢慢地，安全装备成为了橄榄球的标准配置。球员们开始佩戴护肩和面罩，一些勇敢的人发明了第一个橄榄球头盔，尽管并不清楚那个人到底是谁。

16
00:07:57.000 --> 00:08:35.000
One popular story says, as the inventor of the helmet was a Navy Admiral named Joseph Mason Reeves. According to legend, Admiral Reeves was advised by a doctor to stop playing football because he was developing a totally wild medical diagnosis called instant insanity. So Admiral Reeves went to a local shoemaker and had the man fabricate a moleskin cap to protect his cranium. Noah Cohan says, yeah, that's a nice, clean origin story, but we actually don't know who came up with it. There's one version of the story that, in fact, James Naismith, the inventor of basketball, fashioned an early, crude sort of helmet for himself.
一个流行的故事说，头盔的发明者是一位名叫约瑟夫·梅森·里夫斯的海军上将。根据传说，医生建议里夫斯上将停止踢橄榄球，因为他患上了一种完全疯狂的医学诊断，叫做“瞬间疯狂”。于是里夫斯上将去找了一位当地的鞋匠，让那个人制作了一顶鼹鼠皮帽子来保护他的头骨。诺亚·科恩说，是的，那是一个不错、干净的起源故事，但我们实际上并不知道是谁想出来的。有一个版本的说法是，事实上，篮球的发明者詹姆斯·奈史密斯为自己制作了一个早期的、粗糙的头盔。

17
00:08:35.000 --> 00:09:04.000
So some people say he came up with it. But really, it's a kind of convergent evolution. Lots of people are hitting their heads, so lots of people are trying to figure out ways to protect them. Wherever they came from, football helmets became commonplace. But they didn't become mandatory in college football until 1939. The first helmets were made out of leather, and they were designed to be hard and rigid like a shoe. Waring these early helmets was definitely better than playing football with a bare head,
所以有些人说他是发明者。但实际上，这是一种趋同进化。很多人都在撞击头部，所以很多人都在试图想出保护头部的方法。无论它们来自哪里，橄榄球头盔都变得司空见惯。但在1939年之前，它们在大学橄榄球中并不是强制性的。第一批头盔是用皮革制成的，它们被设计得像鞋子一样坚硬和僵硬。佩戴这些早期头盔肯定比赤手空拳踢橄榄球要好，

18
00:09:04.000 --> 00:09:33.000
but the helmets were far from perfect. So even if you were wearing a leather helmet, there were multiple players who died effectively from their skull caving in. They were hit so hard and the leather was not strong enough that the skull would break and part of it would poke into their brain and then this could be almost instantly fatal. So the leather helmet does provide protection, but it's not really a permanent solution, and that's part of the reason the plastic helmet enters the picture.
但头盔远非完美。所以即使你戴着皮头盔，也有多名球员实际上因为头骨塌陷而死亡。他们被撞得太狠了，而皮革不够坚固，导致头骨破裂，部分碎片刺入大脑，这几乎是瞬间致命的。所以皮头盔确实提供了保护，但它并不是一个永久的解决方案，这也是塑料头盔进入视野的部分原因。

19
00:09:33.000 --> 00:09:56.000
The first plastic helmets were developed in the late 1930s by a company called Riddell. Their founder, John Riddell, fabricated helmets for the American military during World War II. And when the war ended, the company went back to making football helmets from a plastic compound called Tenite. Riddell's design was basically a round, hard dome with a chin strap.
第一批塑料头盔是在20世纪30年代末由一家名为Riddell的公司开发的。他们的创始人约翰·里德尔在二战期间为美国军队制造头盔。战争结束后，该公司又回到了使用一种名为Tenite的塑料化合物制造橄榄球头盔的业务。里德尔的设计基本上是一个圆形的、坚硬的圆顶，配有下巴带。

20
00:09:56.000 --> 00:10:34.000
It was clear right away. These new plastic helmets were really good at preventing the kinds of injuries that had been plaguing college football. Today, when we think about football safety, the first thing that comes to mind is probably concussions. But back then, there wasn't as much talk about those. Usually people would use euphemisms like, he got his bell rung, or he's seeing stars. But there was much less public awareness about brain trauma in football. Instead, most of the talk around football safety was centered on brakes, specifically skull fractures. And the plastic helmet was great at preventing those.
显而易见，这些新型塑料头盔在预防一直困扰大学橄榄球的伤害方面非常有效。今天，当我们想到橄榄球安全时，首先想到的可能是脑震荡。但在当时，关于这方面的讨论并不多。通常人们会使用委婉语，比如“他被打得头晕眼花”，或者“他眼冒金星”。但公众对橄榄球中的脑外伤的认识要少得多。相反，关于橄榄球安全的大部分讨论都集中在骨折上，特别是颅骨骨折。而塑料头盔在预防这些方面非常出色。

21
00:10:34.000 --> 00:11:08.000
What I mentioned earlier with the leather helmet caving in, it does not happen when you're wearing a plastic helmet. But despite this safety innovation, the plastic helmets were also very controversial, because they actually made other aspects of the game less safe. the plastic helmet could be used as a weapon. Weirdly, the effort to make the game safer made parts of it more violent. When that player's helmeted skull goes crashing into another player's knee, that blows out their knee, right? So the extremities are impacted
我之前提到的皮头盔塌陷的情况，在戴塑料头盔时就不会发生。但尽管有这种安全创新，塑料头盔也非常有争议，因为它们实际上使比赛的其他方面变得不那么安全。塑料头盔可以被用作武器。奇怪的是，让比赛更安全的努力反而使比赛的部分内容变得更加暴力。当球员戴着头盔的头骨撞向另一名球员的膝盖时，那会摧毁他们的膝盖，对吧？所以四肢受到了影响，

22
00:11:08.000 --> 00:11:41.000
by the plastic helmet becomes a source of safety concern. There was this moment, this really interesting debate. Like, should we go back to leather? Because it now seems like we're having other kinds of risks. Kathleen Bischinski is a public health professor at Ullemberg College, an author of No Game for Boys to Play, about the History of Youth Football. Players are playing in a more dangerous way. If you're, you know, getting hit with a plastic helmet, the other person might be more injured in a way that they wouldn't have with leather. Some colleges were fierce holdouts.
塑料头盔成为了安全隐患的来源。当时有这样一个时刻，一场非常有趣的辩论。比如，我们应该回到皮革时代吗？因为现在看起来我们面临着其他类型的风险。凯瑟琳·比辛斯基是乌伦伯格学院的公共卫生教授，也是《不适合男孩玩的游戏》一书的作者，该书讲述了青少年橄榄球的历史。球员们正在以一种更危险的方式比赛。如果你被塑料头盔撞到，对方可能会受到比使用皮革头盔时更严重的伤害。一些大学是坚定的抵制者。

23
00:11:41.000 --> 00:12:19.000
Notre Dame still had leather helmets into the 1950s. But in the end, football coaches in the NCAA decided the priority had to be pulled out. preventing head injuries. So plastic helmets became the norm. And Noah Cohan says their success wasn't just because of safety concerns. But Redell recognizes that there's something different going on with the aesthetics of the helmet when they switched to plastic. From their very earliest ads, from like a 1940 catalog, they talk about how the helmet is more beautiful when it's fashioned from plastic. The plastic helmets offered a lot of design possibilities.
圣母大学直到20世纪50年代还在使用皮头盔。但最终，橄榄球教练和NCAA决定，首要任务必须是预防头部受伤。因此，塑料头盔成为了常态。诺亚·科恩说，它们的成功不仅仅是因为安全考虑。但里德尔认识到，当他们改用塑料时，头盔的美学发生了变化。从他们最早的广告，比如1940年的目录中，他们谈到了头盔用塑料制成时是如何更美观的。塑料头盔提供了很多设计可能性。

